  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Sunday...\n\nThere are already a lot of mid level clouds building in across \nthe CWA associated with lingering convection. This combined with \nthe wildfire smoke will play a role in limiting our temperatures \ntoday. The extent to which the temperatures will be suppressed is\nstill a question due to the fact that the smoke and clouds are \nnot uniform and breaks in either or both could result in quick \nheating. \n\nA shortwave trough is forecast to push through the area similar\nto what happened yesterday. Showers and thunderstorms are \nforecast to form over the mountains and move off the terrain this \nafternoon. Stronger storms are possible in extreme southeast MT \nwhere the temperatures may be hotter with a slight increase in \nmid-level moisture. Inverted-V forecast soundings suggest a \nprimarily wind threat and with saturated mid levels, wet \nmicrobursts are possible in the SPC marginal risk area this \nafternoon. The surface temperature question posed above will play \na role in convection later today for the marginal area. If we are \nunable to heat up enoug then there will probably be much less \ncoverage in storms that will require the main CVA ahead of the \ntrough to initiate. Bulk wind sheer is slightly better today with \nmuch of the CWA forecast to be between 20-30 kts which, in theory,\nshould support storm maintenance more than we saw yesterday. Long\nstraight hodographs with good cloud level moisture will support \nthe upscale growth of storm clusters as they progress eastwards. \n\nThere continues to be no progressive flow aloft through the \nentire work week and thus the ridge shows little to no signs of \nchanging for several days. Highs in the 90s to 100s appear likely \nto stick around into for the remainder of July. This could pull \nsome monsoonal moisture up into the region with the chance for \nsome showers and storms each day, particularly near the mountains, \nhowever coverage will be scattered any given day therefore\nresulting in a low chance for wetting rain. That being said, \nclimatologically high PWATS will allow for downpours under the \nstronger storms resulting in an very quick wetting rain for those \nlocalized areas. Additionally, wildfire smoke from the several \ncomplexes in Eastern OR will also continue to be around the area \ndue to the predominately westerly flow. This smoke, or lack of \nsmoke, will play a large role in our temperatures over the next \nseveral days.\n\nModels are now showing an increase in moisture over the eastern \n1/4 of the CWA into the middle of the week which could turn into a\nheavy rain and marginal severe weather risk every day through \nFriday. This will need to be monitored as wildfire smoke, the \nstrength of the monsoonal flow, the position and strength of the\nvort maxes, and just how much surface moisture is advected out of\nthe plains are all variables that we still have questions around.\n\nThere continues to be a signal that a ridge crasher event may\nimpact our area next weekend which would usher in cooler temps \nbehind it. This type of event would also bring the potential for \ngusty winds and maybe some storms as well which would be a\npotential concern for fire weather after a prolonged stretch of\nhot and quasi-dry conditions. WMR",
